  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nAnother Warm Day Today, Cloudier and Cooler Into Early Next \nWeek:\n\nAfter another day of anomalous warmth with daytime highs in the mid \nto upper 70s, a persistently mostly cloudy and slightly cooler \nforecast expected through early next week. A narrow band of\nincreased low level moisture along a lower level stretching \naxis of dilatation resides near and over the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley through Monday before a subsequent surge of return \nflow accompanying the upper level low eventually lifting through\nthe Central Plains by midweek advects over the local forecast \narea.\n\nFor Saturday night, band of increased low level moisture looks\nto continue to remain largely to our north and west over the\nUpper Mississippi River Valley. With a cold front stalling to\nour northwest as well, a few showers may (20-40%) develop as\nupper shortwaves eject downstream from the upper low but any \nprecip amounts will be very light.\n\nNext Week: Greater Precipitation Potential, Trending Cooler\n\nThe stalled cold front begins to slowly sag southward late\nSunday into early next week, allowing cooler air to filter into\nthe region. There's still some uncertainty regarding what\ntemperatures will ultimately be due to variations in how far\nsouth the boundary makes it and the influence from potential\nrainfall, but the 24.01z NBM mean suggests highs top out in the\nupper 50s to near 70.\n\nLate Sunday into Monday, the cutoff low over the Desert \nSouthwest begins to move northward, prompting a surface low to \ndevelop across the Great Plains. The various ensemble suites \n(GEFS/EPS/GEPS) appear to be in a bit better agreement about how\nthese features evolve early next week though there is still \nsome variation regarding the track of these features.\n\nCurrently, the ECMWF/Canadian suites suggest these features \ntake a more northern trajectory resulting in lower rainfall \namounts across our area whereas the GFS takes a more southerly \ntrack, bringing greater rainfall amounts to the Mississippi \nRiver Valley. That said, the GFS continues to trend towards an \nECMWF/Canadian solution, so thinking that our western and \nnorthern counties will be the ones to see the bulk of any \nprecipitation. Around 60% of the 23.12z LREF members favor this\nsolution which is in agreement with the 24.01z NBM that \nsuggests 30-50% probabilities for rainfall for these counties. \nRegardless, not expecting a washout by any means. Deterministic \namounts range from 0.25-0.5 inches in the GFS/AIGFS and \nEuro/AIFS with some potential for amounts greater than 0.5 \ninches per the NBM, around 20%. However, given the favored \nnorthern track, the greatest potential for amounts higher than \n0.5 inches exist across central Minnesota, out of our area. \n\nMidweek, there's some modest agreement that a shortwave trough \nmoves across the northern United States, phasing with a longwave\ntrough across Canada. A surface low pressure system looks to \ndevelop across the Midwest in response to these features \nbringing some additional rainfall potential, currently a 20-40% \nchance per the 24.01z NBM, and possibly some breezy winds \nbefore high pressure and dry conditions return by late next \nweek.",
